  "body_html": "<h1>Envy-Free Allocation</h1>\n<p>For a real-valued utility profile $u : N \\to S \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and an\nallocation $A : N \\to S$ (<a class=\"node-ref\" data-node-id=\"social_choice.fair_division.allocation\" href=\"fair_division/social_choice_fair_division_allocation.html\">Allocation</a>),\n$A$ is <em>envy-free</em> (EF) if every agent weakly prefers their own share to\nevery other agent's share:\n$$\n\\forall i, j \\in N,\\ u_i(A(j)) \\le u_i(A(i)).\n$$</p>\n<p>In Lean: <a class=\"lean-decl-ref\" href=\"https://github.com/gametheoryinlean/EconCSLib/blob/7c9a1688acedefe48ec89a442571f12d87a380db/EconCSLib/SocialChoice/FairDivision/Fairness.lean#L26\"><code>SocialChoice.FairDivision.IsEnvyFree</code></a>. The signature takes\n$u$ and $A$ as plain functions, so the predicate applies uniformly to the\ndivisible and indivisible specializations\n(<a class=\"node-ref\" data-node-id=\"social_choice.fair_division.divisible.envy_free\" href=\"fair_division/divisible/social_choice_fair_division_divisible_envy_free.html\">Divisible Envy-Free, Proportional, Equitable Predicates</a>,\n<a class=\"node-ref\" data-node-id=\"social_choice.fair_division.indivisible.envy_free\" href=\"fair_division/indivisible/social_choice_fair_division_indivisible_envy_free.html\">Indivisible Envy-Free</a>).</p>\n<h2>Existence</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Divisible</em> (cake cutting with non-atomic finite measures): EF\n  allocations always exist (Stromquist,\n  <a class=\"node-ref\" data-node-id=\"social_choice.fair_division.divisible.ef_exists\" href=\"fair_division/divisible/social_choice_fair_division_divisible_ef_exists.html\">Envy-Free Existence (Stromquist, n Agents)</a>).</li>\n<li><em>Indivisible</em> (additive valuations over a finite set of items): EF\n  <em>need not</em> exist, even with 2 agents and 1 item\n  (<a class=\"node-ref\" data-node-id=\"social_choice.fair_division.indivisible.ef_impossible_two_agents_one_good\" href=\"fair_division/indivisible/social_choice_fair_division_indivisible_ef_impossible_two_agents_one_good.html\">EF Impossibility — Two Agents, One Good</a>).\n  This motivates the relaxations EF1 / EFX\n  (<a class=\"node-ref\" data-node-id=\"social_choice.fair_division.indivisible.ef1\" href=\"fair_division/indivisible/social_choice_fair_division_indivisible_ef1.html\">EF1 — Envy-Free Up to One Good</a>,\n  <a class=\"node-ref\" data-node-id=\"social_choice.fair_division.indivisible.efx\" href=\"fair_division/indivisible/social_choice_fair_division_indivisible_efx.html\">EFX — Envy-Free Up to Any Good</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>References</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>[AGT Chapter 11] Nisan, Roughgarden, Tardos, and Vazirani, <em>Algorithmic Game Theory</em>. Envy-freeness in fair division.</li>\n</ul>",
